---
type: statement
kind: remark
---

- The concept of a probability measure should be invariant of the implementation of the underlying pseudorandom number generator.
  Meanwhile, a probability measure should only have one nominal value type it randomly generates.
  This is why the pseudorandom number generator `R` is a generic and `Sample` is an associated type.
  ```rs
  // With `Sample` being an associated type, `RandomPokerHand` samples `[Card; 5]`.
  impl<R: Rng> ProbabilityMeasure<R> for RandomPokerHand {
      type Sample = [Card; 5];
      fn sample(&self, rng: &mut R) -> Self::Sample {
          // implementation over any Rng will sample 5 cards; i.e. [Card; 5]
      }
  }
  ```
- Note that the `sample` method only takes an immutable reference to self.
  This means that any impurities in the implementation are coming solely from the pseudorandom number generator.
  We want this behavior, because the probability measure is intended to reflect a mathematics object, and mathematics operations are always pure.
